# .clinerules

Project-specific operating knowledge for AI coding agents. `AGENTS.md` holds the
standing policy; this file holds the facts that are easy to get wrong and cost
real time to rediscover.

## This is an Android app, not a bot

Despite the repository name, this is a **native Android application**: Kotlin,
Jetpack Compose, Room, WorkManager, TDLib via JNI. There is no `bot.py`, no
`requirements.txt`, and no Google Drive integration. Do not scaffold Python.

## Verify on Windows

`verify-project.sh` needs Git Bash, which is often absent. Use the PowerShell port:

```powershell
$env:ANDROID_HOME="$env:LOCALAPPDATA\Android\Sdk"
.\scripts\verify-project.ps1 -Mode QUICK
```

It clears every module's `build/test-results` first, so the run always reports
outcomes it produced. Do not "optimise" that away.

## Build output location

On Windows the `app` module redirects `buildDir` to
`%TEMP%\tdg-build\app\build` as a documented OneDrive reparse-point workaround.
The debug APK is **not** under `app/build`:

```
%TEMP%\tdg-build\app\build\outputs\apk\debug\app-debug.apk
```

The same redirect is expected for `android-release.yml` and is documented in
`docs/architecture/adr-001-tdlib-binaries.md`.

## Coverage

Kover is applied to `:feature` only. `:data` deliberately has no coverage plugin
and is **unmeasured**, not zero. Do not quote a number for `:data`.

```powershell
.\gradlew.bat :feature:koverHtmlReport :feature:koverXmlReport --rerun-tasks
```

`--rerun-tasks` is required. Without it Gradle reports `FROM-CACHE` and hands
back a stale artifact. Measured `:feature`: **38.6% line** (2067/5349),
**25.8% branch**.

## Colour tokens

`AppColors` delegates to `LightColorScheme` in `Theme.kt`; it does not restate
hex values. Read the role before using it:

- `onPrimary` is `Color.White` in the light scheme. It means "content placed on
  top of `primary`", and it is currently **white on a pale container**, which is
  why NavigationBar selection and `EmptyState` buttons look invisible. Prefer
  `onPrimaryContainer` (11.9:1 on `primaryContainer`) or `onSurface` (16.6:1).
- `secondaryContainerLow/High/Highest` were renamed to the real Material roles
  `surfaceContainerLow/High/Highest`.

Do not reintroduce literal colours into `AppColors`; that is what allowed the
palette to drift out of sync with the theme in the first place.

## Native binaries

69.4 MB of TDLib and OpenSSL `.so` files are committed on purpose, for bounded
offline reproducible builds. `AGENTS.md` forbids committing build products of
*this* project; third-party prebuilt libraries are the documented exception.

## Windows PowerShell traps

These have all caused false conclusions in this repository:

- `@{}` hashtables, `Group-Object`, `-eq`, `-match` and `-notmatch` are **case
  insensitive**. Treating `flow.Flow` and `flow.flow` as duplicates deleted a
  required import and broke `:data:compileDebugKotlin`. Use
  `StringComparer.Ordinal` or `-ceq` / `-clike` / `-cmatch`.
- `Remove-Item` on a path relative to the shell can resolve against
  `[Environment]::CurrentDirectory`, which PowerShell does not update when you
  `cd`. Use absolute paths.
- `Select-Object -Last N` at the end of a pipeline buffers the whole stream, so a
  long build shows nothing until it finishes. Write to a log file and read that.

## Reading results honestly

- A task reported `UP-TO-DATE` or `FROM-CACHE` is **not** evidence. Check the
  artifact timestamp.
- Gradle reads cached test outcomes through `SerializableTestResult` before
  running anything. A corrupt cache there fails with
  `IllegalArgumentException: Illegal Capacity: <negative>` and looks exactly like
  a real failure. It is not caused by your change.
- A test count read from files that predate your change proves nothing about
  today.

## Device work

```powershell
adb devices                       # list serials
$env:ANDROID_SERIAL='<serial>'    # always target explicitly when more than one device is attached
.\gradlew.bat :app:installDebug
```

Confirm the ABI matches: this APK ships `arm64-v8a`, `armeabi-v7a`, `x86_64`.
TDLib loads successfully on a physical device; the bundled x86_64 AVD may not
boot on a machine without GPU/GL support, which is an emulator limitation and not
an application fault.
